editorialMd:
Build shared

`drawers` contains two lists of numbers. Find the numbers that appear in both lists. Put each matching number in `shared` only once. Sort `shared` from smallest to largest.

Return the answer

Find the largest number in `shared`. Store it in `largest`. Complete `sharedDrawersPeakTicket` so it returns `largest`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `shared` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n + d log d); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the combined length of the two lists; d is the number of different values. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function sharedDrawersPeakTicket(drawers) {
  const a=drawers; let out=[];
  const right=new Set(a[1]);out=[...new Set(a[0])].filter(x=>right.has(x)).sort((x,y)=>x-y);
  let answer=out.length?out[0]:0;for(const x of out)if(x>answer)answer=x;return answer;
}


